course: world
lesson: 19
title: Mixtures and how to separate them
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: A mixture is two or more substances mixed together without becoming a new substance; each keeps its own properties, so we can separate them again.
explain: Sieving separates pieces of different sizes: small bits fall through the holes and big ones stay behind.
explain: Filtering separates an undissolved solid from a liquid: the liquid passes through the tiny holes in filter paper or cloth, and the solid stays on it.
explain: Evaporating separates a dissolved solid from a liquid: the water leaves as vapour and the solid is left behind, as when salt is made from seawater.
explain: Other tricks use other properties: a magnet pulls iron out of sand, oil floats on water and can be skimmed off, and heavy bits settle to the bottom of still water.
explain: For example, to get salt from muddy salty water you first filter out the mud, then evaporate the water to leave the salt.
fact: In a mixture each substance keeps its own properties.
fact: A sieve separates solids of different sizes.
fact: A filter separates an undissolved solid from a liquid.
fact: Filtering cannot remove dissolved salt or sugar, because the dissolved particles pass through the filter.
fact: Evaporation leaves a dissolved solid behind.
fact: A magnet separates iron and steel from other materials.
fact: Oil and water do not mix; oil floats on top.
fact: Heavy solid bits settle to the bottom of still water, so clearer water can be poured off the top.
fact: To get pure water from salty water, the vapour must be collected and cooled so it condenses; this is called distillation.
fact: Making a mixture is a change that can be reversed; burning is not.
exercise: A cook wants to get the lumps out of a bag of flour. What should she use? | A sieve | A magnet | Evaporation | Boiling
exercise: How would you separate sand from water? | Filter it through paper or cloth | Pull the sand out with a magnet | Pour it through a sieve with big holes | Stir it faster and faster
exercise: How would you get salt back from salty water? | Evaporate the water | Filter it through paper | Sieve it | Shake it hard
exercise: Why can't filter paper take the salt out of salty water? | Dissolved salt particles are small enough to pass through | Salt grains are too big to fit through the paper | Salt sticks to the paper and blocks it | Filter paper soon melts away when it gets wet with salty water
exercise: Iron filings are mixed with sand. What is the easiest way to separate them? | Pull the iron out with a magnet | Pour the mix through filter paper | Dissolve the sand in water | Heat it until the iron melts
exercise: Oil has been spilt into a bowl of water. How can you take most of it out? | Skim the oil off the top, where it floats | Pour it through a sieve | Wait for the oil to dissolve | Stir it hard until all the oil sinks to the bottom
exercise: How is a tea bag like a filter? | Water and the dissolved taste pass through, the leaves stay inside | The bag dissolves the tea leaves | The bag lets the leaves out into the cup | The bag heats the cold water up by itself and slowly turns it into tea
exercise: Which of these is a mixture that can be separated again? | Rice mixed with dried peas | A piece of toast burnt black | A sponge cake baked in the oven | The grey ash left from a fire
exercise: Which method separates mud from muddy water? | Filtering | Using a magnet | Sieving with big holes | Shaking it hard
exercise: Which steps get clean salt from a mixture of salt and sand? | Add water and stir, filter, then evaporate | Evaporate first, then add water | Filter the dry mixture first, then add water to it | Add water, evaporate, then filter
exercise: How can sailors get fresh water from seawater? | Boil it and cool the vapour so it condenses | Filter it through a cloth | Stir it hard until all the salt sinks to the bottom | Let it settle overnight
exercise: Why does a colander, a bowl full of holes, work for draining pasta? | Its holes let the water through but not the pasta | It dissolves the water away | It makes the water evaporate | Its metal pulls the pasta out and leaves the water behind
exercise: A jar of muddy river water is left standing still. What happens? | The heavy bits settle to the bottom | All the mud dissolves | The mud floats up to the top | The water turns into mud
exercise: Is mixing sugar into water a change that can be reversed? | Yes, the sugar can be got back by evaporating the water | No, the sugar is gone for ever | Only if you add salt to it | Only if you freeze it solid and then pick all the sugar out
test: Gold-seekers swirl river gravel in a pan of water. The heavy gold stays in the pan and the lighter sand washes out. Which property is used? | Gold is much denser than sand | Gold dissolves in water | Gold is pulled by magnets | Gold floats on water
test: A boy spills steel paper clips into a bowl of rice. How can he get them out quickly? | Use a magnet | Pour in water and filter it | Dissolve the rice in water | Boil the rice until it is soft
test: Seawater is left in a dish in the hot sunshine. Later there is a white crust. Why? | The water evaporated and left the salt | The dish made salt of its own | The Sun turned the water white | Frost formed on the dish
test: A gardener wants to remove the small stones from a pile of soil. Which tool helps most? | A garden sieve | A sheet of filter paper | A strong magnet | A long thermometer
test: Which cannot be separated by filtering? | Sugar dissolved in tea | Tea leaves floating in tea | Sand stirred into water | Chalk dust mixed into water
test: Why do people let muddy water stand for a while before pouring it off carefully? | The mud settles, so clearer water can be poured off | The mud dissolves while it stands | The water cleans itself in the sunshine as it stands there | Standing water kills all the germs
test: Which is true about a mixture of salt and pepper? | Each keeps its own taste and look | They join into a new substance | The pepper dissolves the salt | It can never be separated
test: A girl wants pure water from salty water, not just the salt. What must she add to evaporating it? | A cold surface to catch the vapour and condense it | A piece of filter paper to catch the salt as it boils | A strong magnet | More salt
test: Why is a paper filter used when making coffee? | It lets the liquid through and holds back the grounds | It takes out all the coffee taste and leaves only water | It dissolves the coffee grounds | It makes the coffee cold
test: how'd u get the sand out of the water | Pour it through a filter | Stir it until the sand dissolves | Use a magnet | Freeze it and the sand floats
test: got iron filings mixed with sand, how to split em | Use a magnet | Use a filter | Add water and boil it | Pour it through a fine sieve
test: my sister says u can get the salt out of salty water with a filter. true? | No, dissolved salt passes through; evaporate the water instead | Yes, a fine filter catches all the salt | Yes, if you filter it twice | No, you need a magnet for salt
test: Mia says oil n water mix if u shake hard enough and stay mixed. right? | No, they separate again and the oil floats on top | Yes, shaking makes them one liquid | Yes, the oil dissolves after a while | No, the water floats on top of the oil
exam: Identify the appropriate method for the separation of sand from water. | Filtration | Use of a magnet | Use of a sieve with large holes | Dissolution
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the filtration of salt water. | The dissolved salt passes through the filter with the water | The salt is retained upon the filter paper | The filter converts the salt into sand | The water is retained and the salt passes through
exam: Identify the process by which pure water may be obtained from salt water. | Distillation, the vapour being collected and condensed | Filtration through fine paper | Separation by means of a magnet | Sieving through a fine mesh
exam: Determine the appropriate method for the separation of iron filings from sand. | The application of a magnet | The addition of water and stirring | Filtration through paper | Evaporation by heating
